A||||3||btoth||what brand EGR valve should i get??||||1||
Z||000000||btoth||10-24-2000||12:40 PM||||i've been meaning to replace my EGR valve and went to the local parts store (Advanced Auto) and they carry one made by GP Sorensen.  I bought it (my friend works there and gets a discount), but it's made to fit a whole variety of cars... would i just be better off getting an AC Delco brand one from the dealership or does it not really matter?? TIA!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-24-2000||12:52 PM||||Probably doesn't matter.  I used a generic EGR valve until the GM came in. I put the GM one in kept the other for a spare.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||10-24-2000||01:11 PM||||Dealer or any other place, but get the AC Delco.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-24-2000||07:22 PM||||If the one that's one there now is the original..... There's information on it that you need to record someplace. The unversal ones come with a label for the number but it fades off. make sure the number is written into someplace more permanant.<P>Otherwise it doens't matter at all which way you go. The main thing is that the meter washer on the universal one must be staked in place. DO NOT rely on the gasket to hold the washer in place. <P>Put the meter washer in place and use a hammer and center punch or old screw driver or corner of a chisle to dent the EGR base in at least 3 places around the edge of the washer. If that washer fall out you could have a major mess on your hands.<P>it's not required but I usually paint the area the washer sets into with Permatex Super 300 or Balkamp Aviation sealer to make a total seal. a very thin film of RTV will also work.||||reg||1
